 # Grid Traversal and Data Structure Adaptation
 
-Also called: Adaptive Isosurface Extraction, Hierarchical Grid Traversal
+Also called: Adaptive Grid Traversal, Hierarchical Isosurface Extraction
 
 ## Overview
-This family adapts standard isosurface extraction (typically Marching Cubes) to non-uniform or sparse data structures, optimizing memory usage and computational cost by focusing resolution only where the surface exists. It differs from uniform grid methods by dynamically determining which cells to evaluate based on spatial hierarchy or sparsity, and from crack-free AMR methods by relying on specific traversal or stitching logic to handle discontinuities between varying resolution levels [S1].
+This family encompasses methods that adapt the domain traversal and data structures of isosurface extraction to handle sparse, hierarchical, or multi-resolution volumetric data, avoiding the memory and computational costs of dense regular grids. Unlike standard grid-based methods that iterate over fixed arrays, these techniques traverse octrees, sparse point sets, or adaptive mesh refinement (AMR) boundaries, dynamically gathering scalar values and generating geometry only where necessary [S1]. They are distinguished from their siblings by their specific approach to handling topological discontinuities: [Marching Cubes with Octree](1259-marching-cubes-with-octree.md) relies on uniform node sizing to prevent cracks, [Sparse Marching Cubes](3825-sparse-marching-cubes.md) uses dynamic lookup for scattered points, [Hierarchical Marching Cubes Enumeration](5223-hierarchical-marching-cubes-enumeration.md) explicitly separates traversal logic for faces, edges, and vertices to ensure coherence, and [2D Stitching](10249-2d-stitching.md) generates stitch cells to bridge AMR level gaps without subdividing quadrilaterals.
 
